/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The CKP faces a toothed wheel on the crankshaft pulley or the flywheel.
   - The CMP reads a single tooth, window or magnetic target on the camshaft.
   - Angular position is an angle, usually measured in degrees before top dead centre (BTDC).
   - n = 1 / (z × T) rpm = 60 / (z × T) Δθ = 360° / z
   - n is revolutions per second; rpm is revolutions per minute; z is the number of tooth pitches per revolution (60 for a 60-2 wheel, 36 for a 36-1 wheel); T is the time between successive normal tooth edges in seconds; Δθ is the crank angle pe
   - A plain toothed wheel gives speed but not absolute position: the ECU cannot tell tooth 1 from tooth 20 after switch-on.
   - The missing-tooth gap for a 60-2 wheel is three tooth periods, not two.
   - Speed comes from the period, not from counting pulses in a fixed window.
   - A variable-reluctance (VR) sensor, also called an inductive sensor, has a permanent magnet and a coil.
   - A Hall sensor has a Hall element, a magnet and a rotating vane or toothed target.
*/
